353:Agreement at finite height does not imply I=K in all heights. The recurrence constructs an exact search for a first additional relation, but supplies no uniform bound on where a first relation would occur if one exists. A proof of the book conjecture requires an all-height argument for every nonsymmetrizable GCM, not three matrices and not a finite-dimensional truncation. Conversely a modular discrepancy needs exact characteristic-zero confirmation and explicit witness verification before it can be called a counterexample.

