   191	## 10. A finite-height certificate controls an infinite coefficient band
   192	
   193	**Theorem.** Fix an index i, let J be its complement, and suppose B=A_J is invertible over Q with B^(-1) entrywise nonnegative. Put
   194	
   195	    v=-B^(-1) a_Ji >=0,    C_i=1+sum_(j in J) v_j.
   196	
   197	Suppose I_alpha=K_alpha is known for every positive degree of total height at most H. If k>=0 is an integer satisfying floor(C_i k)<=H, then equality holds at **every** positive degree with alpha_i<=k, with no total-height bound.
   198	
   199	**Proof.** Let R=ker(S(A) to g(A)), the extra ideal in the full Serre quotient; use its positive homogeneous components. If there were a defect in this band, choose a nonzero homogeneous x in R with least total height **within the band**. No global minimality outside the band is assumed. All simple lowerings decrease height and remain in the band, and the extra ideal has no simple-root or Cartan component. Thus every lowering kills x. By the integrable sl2 argument of Section 8, A alpha<=0.
   200	
   201	The rows indexed by J give B alpha_J<=-a_Ji alpha_i. Multiplying by the entrywise nonnegative inverse preserves the coordinatewise inequality, yielding alpha_J<=v alpha_i. Consequently the integer height is at most floor(C_i alpha_i)<=floor(C_i k)<=H, contradicting the verified lower-height equality. QED.
   202	
   203	This is a proved transfer from a finite certificate, not extrapolation from samples. The inverse-nonnegative hypothesis is essential to this argument; a symmetrizable but indefinite deletion need not have it.
   204	
   205	For A_one, direct rational inversion gives:
   206	
   207	| omitted index i | coordinates v on its ordered complement | C_i |
   208	|---|---|---|
   209	| 1 | (4/3,5/3) on (2,3) | 4 |
   210	| 2 | (3/2,2) on (1,3) | 9/2 |
   211	| 3 | (1,1) on (1,2) | 3 |
   212	
   213	The already accepted height-12 certificate therefore gives equality throughout the union of bands alpha_1<=3, alpha_2<=2, or alpha_3<=4. An accepted height-13 extension strengthens the middle band to alpha_2<=3, because floor((9/2)*3)=13. Exact matrix data and conditional bounds are recorded by `work/round2_band_bounds.py`; the height assumption must be supplied by its separate accepted Lie-algebra certificate.
   214	
   215	## 11. A purely algebraic coefficient-two theorem for an unbounded family
   216	
   217	**Theorem.** For every pair of integers r,s>=1, let
   218	
