   141	By the Gabber–Kac theorem for the symmetrizable B, S(B)=g(B). Its invariant symmetric bilinear form gives a nondegenerate pairing between each nonzero root space g(B)_alpha and its opposite g(B)_(-alpha). For x in W_alpha, every element of the opposite root space is a linear combination of iterated brackets
   142	
   143	    ad(f_j1) ... ad(f_jm) f_i,   all j_k in J,
   144	
   145	with exactly alpha_j occurrences of each index j in J. This spanning statement follows from Jacobi: a negative bracket word containing exactly one f_i can be rewritten with that f_i innermost and all other factors acting on it.
   146	
   147	Move these adjoint operators across the invariant pairing. Up to a sign, the result is a pairing of f_i with a composition of J-lowerings applied to x. All intermediate vectors stay in W, and the final degree is epsilon_i, whose W component is zero. Hence x pairs to zero with the whole opposite root space. Nondegeneracy gives x=0. Thus W=0 in the slice, proving I_alpha=K_alpha. QED.
   148	
   149	**Rank-three corollary.** Every rank-two GCM is symmetrizable: for [[2,-p],[-r,2]] with p,r>0, diag(r,p) is a symmetrizer; the disconnected case is immediate. Therefore for every rank-three GCM, equality holds at every degree having a zero or one coordinate. Any additional positive relation must have all three coordinates at least two. This argument alone excludes the entire height-at-most-five range and leaves (2,2,2) at height six as the first candidate. Section 9 separately excludes that candidate by an exact polynomial certificate. Neither argument settles all interior degrees.
   150	
   151	The proof does not assume that a singular rank-two Cartan matrix has independent rows. If using the alternative lowest-weight-module proof, a full Levi Cartan realization must be chosen inside the ambient full realization. The comparison proof above avoids that extra representation-theoretic bookkeeping.
   152	
   153	## 8. Necessary condition on a first extra relation
   154	
   155	Suppose the extra ideal tau/I is nonzero, and choose a nonzero homogeneous element x of least positive height. Then every D_i x is zero, by minimality. In the Serre quotient the adjoint actions of e_i and f_i are locally nilpotent: this holds on each Chevalley/Cartan generator by the Serre relations and the sl2 relations, and hence on every finite bracket by the iterated derivation rule. The sl2-submodule generated by the lowest-weight vector x is therefore finite-dimensional. Its lowest h_i-weight is a nonpositive integer. Consequently
   156	
   157	    sum_j a_ij alpha_j <= 0 for every i.
   158	
   159	For a rank-three counterexample, combine this with Section 7: a first extra relation can only occur at an interior degree alpha_j>=2 in the fundamental imaginary cone A alpha<=0. This is a necessary condition, not an existence result. The computed examples include such interior degrees rather than testing only the already-excluded boundary.
   160	
   161	## 9. Computer-assisted theorem: every rank-three GCM agrees through height six
   162	
   163	**Theorem.** For every rank-three GCM in characteristic zero, I_alpha=K_alpha for every positive alpha of total height at most six. In particular no additional defining relation can first occur below height seven in rank three. This is a bounded-height theorem for an unbounded family of matrices, not an all-height theorem for any nonsymmetrizable matrix. Its novelty in the literature has not been established.
